{"parentUuid":"26c65a71-c80d-471c-a2f3-128383c42eba","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2c5eba9785c3363d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01C9iUHKScTkHgWTjbUvNvod","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BULoWS8sfLeqyvcfsHbnYs","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Chuffed lazy clause generation solver MiniZinc benchmark comparison CP-SAT"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7906,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"output_tokens":332,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7906},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":332,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7906,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7906,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoosRXaSpBfhtWEANojG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"696a60cd-c599-43f5-8e9f-4644efd85071","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:08.972Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"26c65a71-c80d-471c-a2f3-128383c42eba","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a2c5eba9785c3363d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ms1cyC6RoqyGkVai5Bbvtg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CpModelProto export CNF DIMACS converter\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/cp_model.proto at main · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/main/ortools/sat/cp_model.proto\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - MassimoLauria/cnftools: A set of efficient tools for CNF manipulations.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/MassimoLauria/cnftools\"},{\"title\":\"DIMACS (cpmpy.tools.dimacs) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/tools_xcsp3/api/tools/dimacs.html\"},{\"title\":\"CpModelProto (com.google.ortools:ortools-java 9.4.1874 API)\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/javadoc/com/google/ortools/sat/CpModelProto.html\"},{\"title\":\"CNF Files\",\"url\":\"https://people.sc.fsu.edu/~jburkardt/data/cnf/cnf.html\"},{\"title\":\"DIMACS CNF - Varisat Manual\",\"url\":\"https://jix.github.io/varisat/manual/0.2.0/formats/dimacs.html\"},{\"title\":\"DIMACS (cpmpy.tools.dimacs) — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/var-comp-sum/api/tools/dimacs.html\"},{\"title\":\"Converter from CNF (Conjunctive-Normal-Form) to Prolog\",\"url\":\"http://www.picat-lang.org/bprolog/publib/cnf2pl.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - easyas314159/cnftools: A collection of tools for working with and generating Dimacs CNF files. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/easyas314159/cnftools\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - MassimoLauria/cnfgen: CNF generator in DIMACS format. It produces common families of CNFs. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/MassimoLauria/cnfgen\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about OR-Tools and DIMACS/CNF formats, but the results don't show a direct built-in converter from OR-Tools CpModelProto to DIMACS CNF format.\n\nHere's what the search revealed:\n\n**About CpModelProto:**\nCpModelProto is a message structure in OR-Tools that defines a constraint programming problem, containing variables, constraints, and an objective.\n\n**About DIMACS CNF Format:**\nThe DIMACS CNF format is a textual representation of a formula in conjunctive normal form - a conjunction of clauses, where each clause is a disjunction of literals (variables or their negations).\n\n**Alternative Approaches:**\nThe search results show that other constraint modeling libraries have DIMACS export capabilities. For example, CPMpy implements helper functions for exporting models to DIMACS format using a \"to_cnf\" transformation.\n\nHowever, the search results do not show that OR-Tools has a built-in CpModelProto to DIMACS CNF converter. If you need this functionality, you may need to:\n1. Check the latest OR-Tools documentation directly\n2. Implement a custom converter\n3. Export your model in a different format and use third-party conversion tools\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d9750afb-8278-4539-b55c-11a72aa17372","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:13.547Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"26c65a71-c80d-471c-a2f3-128383c42eba","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"aaa69bd7-b570-4a0f-b3b8-266b2d7d02f9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a2c5eba9785c3363d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eu5sHb8dkMEMgbRFN11CTH","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PySAT cardinality encodings totalizer sequential encoder API documentation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cardinality encodings (pysat.card) — PySAT 1.9.dev2 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/card.html\"},{\"title\":\"Cardinality encodings (pysat.card) — PySAT 1.8.dev14 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq-github-io.translate.goog/docs/html/api/card.html?_x_tr_sl=en&_x_tr_tl=es&_x_tr_hl=es&_x_tr_pto=tc\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT Documentation Release 1.9.dev2\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/pysat.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean encodings (pysat.pb) — PySAT 1.9.dev2 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/pb.html\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: A Python Toolkit for Prototyping with SAT Oracles\",\"url\":\"https://www.birs.ca/cmo-workshops/2018/18w5208/files/Marques-SilvaJoao2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"pysat/pysat/card.py at master · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/blob/master/pysat/card.py\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - pysathq/pysat: A toolkit for SAT-based prototyping in Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: A Python Toolkit for Prototyping with SAT Oracles ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://alexeyignatiev.github.io/assets/pdf/imms-sat18-preprint.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: SAT technology in Python - Features\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/features/\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: SAT technology in Python\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive documentation on PySAT's cardinality encodings. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe cardinality encoding module provides access to various cardinality constraint encodings to formulas in conjunctive normal form (CNF).\n\n## Available Encodings\n\nThe cardinality encoding types include: pairwise, seqcounter, sortnetwrk, cardnetwrk, bitwise, ladder, totalizer, mtotalizer, kmtotalizer, and native.\n\n## Key Classes\n\n**EncType**: This class represents a C-like enum type for choosing the cardinality encoding to use. The desired encoding can be selected either directly by its integer identifier, or by its alphabetical name, e.g. EncType.sortnetwrk.\n\n**CardEnc**: This abstract class is responsible for the creation of cardinality constraints encoded to a CNF formula. The class has three class methods for creating AtMostK, AtLeastK, and EqualsK constraints. Given a list of literals, an integer bound and an encoding type, each of these methods returns an object of class pysat.formula.CNFPlus representing the resulting CNF formula.\n\n**ITotalizer**: This class implements the iterative totalizer encoding. Note that ITotalizer can be used only for creating AtMostK constraints.\n\n## Documentation Resources\n\nThe official API documentation is available at: https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/card.html\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0d344c5f-0f7c-4270-9955-4c62aba4f4fc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:13.706Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aaa69bd7-b570-4a0f-b3b8-266b2d7d02f9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"cfa42fe0-8ba1-4940-b3d5-5f684cc3c731","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a2c5eba9785c3363d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TJv86GxjXjkMKwpKNkvAmj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Sugar CSP SAT encoder order encoding blow-up factor benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Sugar: A CSP to SAT Translator Based on Order Encoding | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/239541125_Sugar_A_CSP_to_SAT_Translator_Based_on_Order_Encoding\"},{\"title\":\"Sugar: A CSP to SAT Translator Based on Order Encoding\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/CPAI06/descriptionSolvers/Sugar.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Sugar: a SAT-based Constraint Solver - CSPSAT Project\",\"url\":\"https://cspsat.gitlab.io/sugar/\"},{\"title\":\"Azucar: A SAT-Based CSP Solver Using Compact Order Encoding | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-31612-8_37\"},{\"title\":\"Computing Science Group THE ORDER ENCODING: FROM TRACTABLE CSP TO TRACTABLE SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/files/4014/maxclosed_orderencoding_v16_TR.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Hybrid Encoding of CSP to SAT Integrating Order and Log Encodings\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304288091_A_Hybrid_Encoding_of_CSP_to_SAT_Integrating_Order_and_Log_Encodings\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) System description of a SAT-based CSP solver Sugar\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228943427_System_description_of_a_SAT-based_CSP_solver_Sugar\"},{\"title\":\"Azucar: A SAT-Based CSP Solver Using Compact Order Encoding - (Tool Presentation) | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Azucar:-A-SAT-Based-CSP-Solver-Using-Compact-Order-Tanjo-Tamura/e898b50712e538be16cb5a0d1ba33cb3727ebed6\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) The Order Encoding: From Tractable CSP to Tractable SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220944335_The_Order_Encoding_From_Tractable_CSP_to_Tractable_SAT\"},{\"title\":\"Some Tips of Implementing Order Encoding\",\"url\":\"https://cspsat.gitlab.io/sugar/docs/order-encoding/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Sugar, CSP to SAT encoding, order encoding, and the blow-up factor:\n\n## Sugar CSP to SAT Solver\n\nSugar is a SAT-based Constraint Solver that encodes Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP) to Boolean CNF formulas, which are then solved by an external SAT solver. The sugar solver solves a finite linear CSP by translating it into a SAT problem using the order encoding method and then solving the translated SAT problem with the MiniSat solver.\n\n## Order Encoding Method\n\nIn the order encoding method, a comparison x≤a is encoded by a different Boolean variable for each integer variable x and integer value a. The benefit of this encoding is the natural representation of the order relation on integers, with axiom clauses of two literals representing the order relation of an integer variable.\n\n## Encoding Blow-up Factor and Benchmarks\n\nRegarding the blow-up factor, for parameters (9,3,100) the direct and log encodings produced around 19,700 clauses each, whereas the order encoding produced an average of 46,957 clauses. However, Mini-SAT's performance was still much better on the clauses produced by the order encoding even in those cases where this encoding produced a larger set of clauses than the other encodings.\n\nSugar is an award winning solver of global constraint categories at the International CSP Solver Competitions in 2008 and 2009, and of four categories at the 2008 International Max-CSP Solver Competition.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d4ab9c6c-1078-45d4-9245-cd668f50a30b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:15.357Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cfa42fe0-8ba1-4940-b3d5-5f684cc3c731","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"696a60cd-c599-43f5-8e9f-4644efd85071","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a2c5eba9785c3363d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BULoWS8sfLeqyvcfsHbnYs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Chuffed lazy clause generation solver MiniZinc benchmark comparison CP-SAT\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Chuffed, a lazy clause generation solver\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/chuffed/chuffed\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Variable Activity Initialisation for Lazy Clause Generation Solvers | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-78230-6_4\"},{\"title\":\"FreshPorts -- math/chuffed: Lazy clause generation CP solver for MiniZinc\",\"url\":\"https://www.freshports.org/math/chuffed\"},{\"title\":\"3.5. Solving Technologies and Solver Backends — The MiniZinc Handbook 2.9.7\",\"url\":\"https://docs.minizinc.dev/en/stable/solvers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Variable Activity Initialisation for Lazy Clause Generation Solvers | Integration of Constraint Programming, Artificial Intelligence, and Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-030-78230-6_4\"},{\"title\":\"Bug#1063388: ITP: chuffed -- lazy clause generation CP solver\",\"url\":\"https://lists.debian.org/debian-devel/2024/02/msg00078.html\"},{\"title\":\"3.5. Solving Technologies and Solver Backends — The MiniZinc Handbook 2.8.6\",\"url\":\"https://docs.minizinc.dev/en/2.8.6/solvers.html\"},{\"title\":\"SUNNY-CP and the MiniZinc Challenge\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1706.08627\"},{\"title\":\"chuffed/README.md at develop · chuffed/chuffed\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/chuffed/chuffed/blob/develop/README.md\"},{\"title\":\"3.5. Solving Technologies and Solver Backends — The MiniZinc Handbook 2.9.1\",\"url\":\"https://docs.minizinc.dev/en/2.9.1/solvers.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Chuffed, lazy clause generation, solver benchmarks, and their relationship to MiniZinc and CP-SAT:\n\n## Chuffed Solver Overview\n\nChuffed is a state of the art lazy clause solver designed from the ground up with lazy clause generation in mind. Lazy clause generation is a hybrid approach to constraint solving that combines features of finite domain propagation and Boolean satisfiability.\n\n## Architecture and Performance\n\nThe FD components of Chuffed are very tightly integrated with a SAT solver. For \"small\" variables (|D| <= 1000), SAT variables representing [x = v] or [x >= v] are eagerly created at the start of the problem. Channelling constraints are natively enforced by the variable objects in order to keep the FD solver and the SAT solver's view of the domains fully consistent at all times. For \"large\" variables (|D| > 1000), the SAT variables are lazily generated as needed.\n\nThe overhead from lazy clause generation ranges from negligible to perhaps around 100%. The search reduction, however, can reach orders of magnitude on appropriate problems.\n\n## MiniZinc Integration and Benchmarking\n\nChuffed is a constraint solver based on lazy clause generation. This type of solver adapts techniques from SAT solving, such as conflict clause learning, watched literal propagation and activity-based search heuristics, and can often be much faster than traditional CP solvers.\n\nResearch has benchmarked ML-aided Chuffed on the MiniZinc benchmark suite and found a robust 2.5% gain over baseline Chuffed on MRCPSP instances.\n\nThe search results reference MiniZinc Challenges as a resource for performance comparisons between solvers, though specific CP-SAT benchmark comparisons weren't detailed in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"029eef39-e330-4743-bb59-e60e9f499119","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:17.457Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"696a60cd-c599-43f5-8e9f-4644efd85071","userType":"external","e